Piped -p no longer loses held text when its event loop panics. Committed as 31f83097 ("Part of #1016"), and the details are in a comment on #1016, which stays open.

What the probe showed. Before the fix, I built a filtering RetrySafeGate writing into an in-memory buffer, left text held with no MessageEnd, and dropped the gate inside catch_unwind. The buffer read "", so the held bytes really were lost on a panic.

The fix is impl Drop for RetrySafeGate in src/prompt/retry_safe_pipe.rs. It writes only while std::thread::panicking() is true and the filter is on. It writes two things, in order:
• text the gate had already released to the loop but the loop hadn't printed yet;
• then the text still held.

The first part goes slightly beyond the task: when the panic happens in the middle of printing a finished attempt, the rest of that attempt is sitting in the gate's queue and would be lost too. A normal drop writes nothing, the same as before, so an attempt thrown away for a retry can never reach stdout this way. Write errors are ignored, and nothing in Drop can panic.

Tests compare the full bytes with assert_eq!:
• panic while text is held → "HELD_"
• panic with a finished attempt still waiting to print → "ONE "
• normal drop with text held → "" (today's value)
• an attempt discarded for a retry → "", both on a panic and on a normal drop. A control row shows the same failure without the retry does flush PARTIAL_, so the empty result really comes from the retry.
• filter off → "" on a panic

For the positive control I turned the write into a no-op, ran the tests, put it back and ran again, all in one command. The three flush tests failed by name, the near-miss tests stayed green, and nothing was left behind after the restore. Clippy, fmt and the full cargo test run all pass.

Limits:
• I checked by reading the code, not by running yoyo, that a panic actually reaches the gate. The gate is a local variable in handle_prompt_events, and single-shot runs go through #[tokio::main] with no tokio::spawn in between, so a panic in the loop unwinds through it.
• A panic inside yoagent's own spawned agent task isn't covered: the gate just sees the stream end normally.
• Also not covered: a panic after the gate was already dropped normally, and builds with panic = "abort", where Drop never runs.
• The exit code on a panic stays Rust's default, 101.
• `Drop` writes the text raw, without the usual leading-blank-line filter.

Something I found but didn't test: the Ctrl+C and SIGTERM path (finish_interrupted_turn) flushes only the held text, not text waiting in the gate's queue. If a signal arrives while that queue has text in it, that text would likely be lost. That comes from reading the code only, and I noted it on #1016.

I also updated the docs to match: the panic case is now in docs/src/usage/piped-mode.md, the "still open" note in src/prompt/sigterm.rs is corrected, and ARCHITECTURE.md has a new entry with the old claim marked as superseded.
